"1z5ivd4bvgv":"Power requirements define the voltage and current ranges a USB PD-compliant Source must provide, ensuring interoperability across the device ecosystem.",
"90a9j731s6":"SPR Voltage and Current Rules (by PDP)",
"hcn4cpzpar":"Chapter 4. Power Delivery Electrical Requirements",
"ptw5b35pom":"The electrical requirements define how fast and how stable VBUS transitions must be, plus the protection mechanisms.",
"1v1xpj81rkc":"A new voltage must settle within the vSrcNew range in the allowed time, and the rise rate has an upper limit.",
"7ycfvy9uia":"A Source needs three protections: OCP, OVP and OTP.",
"1eg1741e84w":"After a disconnect, VBUS must drop to vSafe5V (4.75–5.5V) or below within tSafe5V (275ms max).",
"6powv900ri":"<strong>Positive transitions</strong>: the new voltage must settle within the <span class=\"highlight\">vSrcNew</span> range in the allowed time, and the maximum slew rate is limited by <span class=\"highlight\">vSrcSlewPos</span>.",
"b9ex2lun7i":"<strong>Protection</strong>: a Source needs <strong>OCP (over-current protection)</strong>, <strong>OVP (over-voltage protection)</strong> and <strong>OTP (over-temperature protection)</strong>.",
"1v2ucrdy7yq":"Chapter 5. Physical Layer",
"b4sqo4xr3i":"PD communication has three layers: the BMC physical layer, the protocol layer and the Policy Engine.",
"11p8f8wypry":"The physical layer (BMC) only turns bits into voltage waveforms on CC and does not care about content.",
"15z4f8yiurx":"The protocol layer builds packets, replies with GoodCRC and retries.",
"29wa5xnzywr":"The Policy Engine runs on the MCU and decides what power to advertise and whether to accept a request.",
"15dzy9i22bx":"BMC details and the encoder tool are in Chapter 6; packet fields are in Chapter 7.",
"1y90391b78":"USB PD uses <strong>Bi-phase Mark Coding (BMC)</strong> as the physical-layer protocol on the CC line.",
"1s7n5hpwe0p":"In Practice: Mapping the BMC PHY and the PD Protocol Engine to the OSI Model",
"2307ewyp4z0":"In a system they sit at different layers and work closely together:",
"1o7mml4jka1":"<strong>[OSI Layer 1] BMC physical layer (PHY):</strong> converts digital 0/1 to and from the actual voltage waveform on the CC line (bit-stream level). Like a postman, it only sends and reads voltage transitions accurately and does not care about content.",
"1a89g0zo1r6":"<strong>[OSI Layer 2/3] PD protocol engine:</strong> handles packet assembly and parsing (Header/Payload), automatic acknowledgment (GoodCRC) and retries. Like an editor, it checks the envelope format and interprets what the command means.",
"t35cbiksu6":"<strong>[OSI Layer 3/4] Policy Engine:</strong> the host MCU sets the power rules and makes the decisions.",
"1fenus8flo7":"Chapter 6. BMC Signal Encoding and Electrical Characteristics",
"188rg0937qh":"BMC is the line code on CC: every bit cell starts with a transition, and a 1 adds one more transition in the middle.",
"23pv7u9plxt":"The rate is 300 kbps (the spec allows 270–330 kbps), so 1 UI is about 3.33µs.",
"ys6bzqx01h":"A real packet is first protected with CRC-32 and encoded with 4b5b, then given a Preamble, SOP* and EOP, and only then converted into a BMC waveform.",
"1332q5zl77s":"The transmitter voltage swing vSwing is 1.05–1.2V, and packets must be at least 25µs apart.",
"1iwgym7oqcu":"Use the encoder below to enter a Header and Data Objects and see the waveform directly.",
"1lp8ng96tki":"Interactive tool: <a href=\"#bmc\">BMC packet encoder</a>",
"1n2l0orgngu":"<strong>Bi-phase Mark Coding (BMC)</strong> is a half-duplex, DC-balanced way of communicating, carried over the CC pin.",
"10pt15en8wu":"In Practice: Encoding Logic and Frequency",
"14hx23kdnfb":"<strong>0/1 encoding logic (transition-based):</strong> every bit cell (1 UI) always starts with a transition. A logic 0 stays unchanged in the middle of the cell, and a logic 1 makes one more transition in the middle.",
"ozmjf50jjr":"<strong>Nominal bit rate and frequency:</strong> the bit rate is 300 kbps (the spec allows 270–330 kbps; 1 UI is about 3.33 μs, with a range of 3.03–3.70 μs). The actual waveform frequency falls between 150 kHz and 300 kHz.",
"1jil5o2dgh5":"<strong>Electrical levels:</strong> the transmitter voltage swing vSwing is 1.05V to 1.2V (1.125V nominal), and the rise/fall times tRise/tFall must not exceed 300ns. Packets must be separated by at least tInterFrameGap of 25μs. The receiver judges the signal against the Rx Mask, whose center line is 0.6875V, 0.4375V or 0.5625V depending on whether the port is sourcing power, sinking power or neither.",
"cwemyalnnz":"Interactive Tool: Encode a PD Packet into a BMC Waveform",
"slw1av38o0":"Enter a Message Header and Data Objects, and the tool builds the complete physical-layer packet step by step as the spec describes: it adds the CRC-32, converts each nibble to a 4b5b symbol, adds the Preamble, SOP* K-codes and EOP, and finally draws the BMC waveform. You can click any bit cell to inspect it, or choose Hard Reset or Cable Reset to see the reset signals, which carry no payload.",
