"a1h7k6bydi":" Show GoodCRC",
"1393gagmfvn":"Blue is a Control Message and purple is a Data Message. The receiver must reply with GoodCRC to every message; the time values are the typical ranges in the spec, so refer to the spec for exact values. To see how the packet fields decode, use the interactive diagram in Chapter 7.",
"2gons0gmrik":"Interactive Tool: PDO/RDO Field Decoder",
"1hvemim1off":"PDOs and RDOs are both 32-bit Data Objects. Here you can load an example, or paste an 8-digit hexadecimal value you captured from Source_Capabilities or Request, and see each field's bit range, raw value, and the converted voltage, current and power. A PDO's type is detected automatically from bits 31..30; an RDO's format depends on which kind of PDO it points to, so choose it yourself.",
"vb1z0itfpl":"PDO or RDO",
"2651n2pm4xh":"PDO (Source's capability advertisement)",
"116x02adin1":"RDO (Sink's request)",
"afg4vj64hf":"Data Object (hex): 0x",
"1ytx7yyochu":"Fixed / Variable RDO",
"1vrzorbsm47":" This is the first PDO of Source_Capabilities (5V Fixed, with flag fields)",
"28gmet0at6h":"Field",
"2680x8q0q9j":"Raw value",
"21sxi2f6cle":"Converted",
"1exta0fxc2t":"This tool only decodes PDOs sent by a Source (the PDO format in Sink_Capabilities differs slightly, for example bits 28..23 of the 5V Fixed PDO). Field definitions follow Table 6.6 to 6.22 of USB PD R3.2 V1.2. The RDO's Maximum Operating Current/Power is deprecated, and the spec requires it to be set equal to Operating.",
"1nlzqlkoi8p":"PPS lets a Sink adjust the voltage finely over a small range and limit current, which suits direct charging of lithium batteries.",
"8kifv3xjuy":"Voltage step is 20mV and current-limit step is 50mA.",
"1ltruzhh754":"A PPS APDO's maximum voltage can only be 11V, 16V or 21V, and the minimum is 5V (3.3V is deprecated).",
"pfycf1sqro":"Two modes: constant voltage (CV) and current limit (CL). In CL the voltage is lowered to avoid triggering OCP.",
"wtgnjefhww":"PPS lets a device adjust voltage and current very finely over a range, to suit the efficient conversion needs of direct lithium-battery charging.",
"1g7o82wma76":"<strong>Voltage step</strong>: <span class=\"highlight\">20mV (vPpsStep)</span>",
"pjyrbkyrnw":"<strong>Current-limit step</strong>: <span class=\"highlight\">50mA (iPpsCLStep)</span>",
"27az8sg2i2q":"PPS supports two operating modes: <strong>Constant Voltage mode (CV)</strong> maintains the output voltage; <strong>Current Limit mode (CL)</strong>, when the Sink draws more current than the set value, actively lowers the voltage to avoid triggering over-current protection (OCP).",
"ujinj58tey":"Chapter 10. The 12V PDO Question",
"e33pj71yqr":"12V is not a standard fixed voltage in the PD spec; the 12V outputs seen on the market are mostly compatibility measures.",
"l2a6z1uzae":"The fixed voltage levels in the spec are 5V, 9V, 15V and 20V.",
"28uwvi727za":"A common 12V comes from compatibility with legacy protocols such as QC, custom non-standard PDOs, or automotive 12V environments.",
"1ihfwzknzl":"The proper approach is to request 12.00V directly with a PPS/AVS APDO.",
"20nnh2tzzkk":"Under the strict Power Rules of the USB PD spec, <strong>12V is not a normative fixed voltage</strong>; the mandated levels are 5V, 9V, 15V and 20V.",
"170q930pett":"In Practice: Why Is 12V Output Still Common?",
"1j3vpdu10v9":"<strong>Cross-protocol compatibility:</strong> many adapters use multi-protocol chips and must support legacy fast-charging protocols such as QC 2.0/3.0 that depend on 12V.",
"10y8ok0j2nj":"<strong>Custom non-standard PDOs:</strong> to stay compatible with legacy 12V DC devices (such as drive enclosures and automotive electronics), vendors write a 12V PDO into the FW.",
"187cqx6cn88":"<strong>Native automotive 12V environments:</strong> a car charger's input is a 12V lead-acid battery, so passing through or converting 12V is the most efficient.",
"1lqjx3jidmb":"<strong>The proper solution (PPS):</strong> a modern Sink can request exactly 12.00V through a PPS/AVS APDO (such as 3.3V–21V) by fine adjustment, fully compliant with the PD 3.0 standard.",
"1cazhinjsxj":"Chapter 11. PD Source and Basic Power Delivery with Rp/Rd",
"fq34so5cbb":"A Source advertises its default current with Rp on CC, and the Sink's Rd divides against it to produce a voltage.",
"1y42dyrumr2":"Default USB, 1.5A and 3.0A correspond to Rp of 56kΩ, 22kΩ and 10kΩ (pulled up to 5V), or to current sources of 80µA, 180µA and 330µA.",
"23dtkda8n0y":"The Sink's Rd is 5.1kΩ, so the CC voltage is about 0.41V, 0.92V and 1.68V, from which the Sink knows how much current it may draw.",
"7qb17dgyr0":"A Source turns on VBUS only after it sees Rd for tCCDebounce (100–200ms).",
"2aj1dnadajp":"With the interactive tool below you can vary the Rp and Rd tolerance and see which range the voltage falls in.",
"28lva2dee5d":"Interactive tool: <a href=\"#cc\">CC voltage detection tool</a>",
"wwd5948kxt":"A <strong>PD Source</strong> is the device that supplies power on the VBUS pin in a USB PD connection. At the initial connection, the Source places a pull-up resistor (Rp) on the CC line. By changing the Rp value, the Source can advertise the default Type-C current, and it also uses Rp as a collision-avoidance mechanism (<code>SinkTxOk</code> / <code>SinkTxNG</code>).",
"7v904ecot6":"Basic Type-C Power Delivery and the Rp/Rd Resistor Relationship",
"c1ynfi2zv":"Before the formal USB PD handshake (Explicit Contract), the Type-C interface relies on the physical-layer <strong>Rp (the Source-side pull-up resistor)</strong> and <strong>Rd (the Sink-side pull-down resistor, fixed at 5.1kΩ)</strong> to set the initial 5V current limit. By measuring the divided voltage on the CC pin, the Sink learns the maximum current the Source allows it to draw. This is the <strong>Default Contract</strong>.",
